{"parentUuid":"9ff8561b-6bdd-44d9-96d7-b138e1e41ea7","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"d942574f-347d-4ecb-b6b4-9710df54d5c6","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a1f1a70","slug":"happy-swimming-fern","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_0136xLjVT5rcjs9jVjtHDjAs","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→--- Page 1 ---\n     2→Temporally multiplexed ion-photon quantum interface via fast ion-chain transport\n     3→Bingran You,1, 2 Qiming Wu,1, 2, ∗ David Miron,1, 2 Wenjun Ke,1\n     4→Inder Monga,2 Erhan Saglamyurek,1, 2 and Hartmut Haeffner1, 2, †\n     5→1Department of Physics, University of California, Berkeley, CA 94270, USA\n     6→2Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory, Berkeley, CA 94720, USA\n     7→(Dated: January 12, 2026)\n     8→High-rate remote entanglement between photon and matter-based qubits is essential for dis\u0002tributed quantum information processing. A key technique to increase the modest entangling rates\n     9→of existing long-distance quantum networking approaches is multiplexing. Here, we demonstrate\n    10→a temporally multiplexed ion-photon interface via rapid transport of a chain of nine calcium ions\n    11→across 74 µm within 86 µs. The non-classical nature of the multiplexed photons is verified by mea\u0002suring the second-order correlation function with an average value of g\n    12→(2)(0) = 0.060(13), indicating\n    13→negligible crosstalk between the multiplexed modes. In addition, we characterize the motional\n    14→degree-of-freedom of the ion crystal after transport and find that it is coherently excited to as much\n    15→as n¯α ≈ 110 for the center-of-mass mode. Our proof-of-principle implementation paves the way\n    16→for large-scale quantum networking with trapped ions, but highlights some challenges that must be\n    17→overcome.\n    18→Remote entanglement across distant quantum nodes [?\n    19→? ] may be used for long-distance quantum key distribu\u0002tion [? ? ], modular quantum computer architectures [?\n    20→? ], as well as quantum enhanced metrology and sens\u0002ing [? ? ? ]. Light-matter quantum interfaces are a fun\u0002damental building block for such applications, and allow\n    21→for distributed entanglement between stationary matter\n    22→qubits by using \"flying\" photons.\n    23→For practical purposes, these quantum interfaces need\n    24→to be capable of establishing remote entanglement at\n    25→high rates across a large-scale network of quantum nodes.\n    26→However, in widely adopted probabilistic schemes based\n    27→on heralded photon detection [? ? ? ], it is infeasible to\n    28→distribute entanglement at useful rates over a few kilo\u0002meters, as the attempt rate is limited by the round-trip\n    29→travel-time of photons in a single mode. For example, for\n    30→a communication distance of 10 km, photon-travel time\n    31→will be around 100 µs, meaning that the attempt can\u0002not be higher than 10 kHz. In this scenario, even with\n    32→use of a state-of-the-art photon-matter interface that can\n    33→yield close to unity photon extraction efficiency [? ], the\n    34→matter-photon entanglement rate beyond tens of kilome\u0002ters would be limited to sub 10 s−1 with total loss around\n    35→15 dB after considering the collective probability of gener\u0002ating and detecting telecom photons at each node, along\n    36→with the optical fiber loss [? ? ].\n    37→A key solution to this challenge is multiplexing: to\n    38→combine multiple signals into a single channel and there\u0002fore increase the attempt rate [? ? ]. Multiplexing has\n    39→become a mature technology for ensemble-based quan\u0002tum interfaces, both in atomic gases and rare-earth ion\n    40→doped solid-state systems [? ? ? ? ]. However, large\u0002scale local quantum information processing is technically\n    41→challenging in these platforms [? ].\n    42→In contrast, single emitters, including trapped ions and\n    43→neutral atoms, offer excellent local quantum information\n    44→processing capability beside their natural interface with\n    45→light at convenient wavelengths for quantum frequency\n    46→conversion (QFC) [? ? ], and the possibility of long\u0002lived storage of entanglement [? ? ]. On the other hand,\n    47→implementing a multiplexed light-matter interface with\n    48→these systems is technically challenging. Towards over\u0002coming this problem, a few multiplexing schemes have\n    49→already been proposed for ion and atom-based quantum\n    50→processors [? ? ? ? ]. The only reported experimental\n    51→work, we are aware of, is the demonstration of multi\u0002plexing using a static three-ion chain [? ]. In view of the\n    52→recent advances of the quantum CCD architecture [? ? ?\n    53→], a complementary approach to multiplexing is the pro\u0002cess of ion-transport through a specific spatial location\n    54→with maximized photon coupling efficiency.\n    55→In this work, we demonstrate a temporal multiplexing\n    56→scheme based on the transport of an ion-chain for improv\u0002ing the rate of ion-photon entanglement over long dis\u0002tances. In our experiments, we generate on-demand sin\u0002gle photons by shuttling a nine-ion chain across the focus\n    57→of a single-ion addressing beam. This scheme is expected\n    58→to lead to a nearly nine-fold increase in attempt rate of\n    59→the entanglement generation for quantum repeater nodes\n    60→separated by >100 km. We verify the single-photon na\u0002ture of the photon trains by measuring a second-order\n    61→time correlation of g\n    62→(2)(0) = 0.060(13) without back\u0002ground subtraction. Furthermore, we address the prob\u0002lem of motional excitation during the transport, which\n    63→is detrimental to local entangling operations [? ] and\n    64→in the case of using a cavity for stimulating the photons\n    65→would lead to uncertainly in the coupling strength. [?\n    66→]. Using a shuttling function designed to mitigate mo\u0002tional excitation, we find coherent excitation of n¯α ∼ 50\n    67→on the center-of-mass (COM) mode during one round of\n    68→ion chain transport. These results show that the pro\u0002posed multiplexing scheme can be scaled up to higher\n    69→rates provided that more optimal transport methods are\n    70→applied.\n    71→\n    72→--- Page 2 ---\n    73→2\n    74→The schematics of the experimental procedures is illus\u0002trated in Fig. ??(a). Our experiment is conducted using\n    75→an RF Paul trap, composed of four RF blades for generat\u0002ing radial pseudopotential and two DC endcap electrodes\n    76→for providing an axial harmonic confinement. We typi\u0002cally trap a string of nine 40Ca+ ions in a linear configu\u0002ration with the COM mode frequency of ωx,z/(2π) = ×\n    77→{1.15, 0.179} MHz and Lamb-Dicke (LD) parameters of\n    78→the axial modes, ranging between 0.09 and 0.23 on the\n    79→4\n    80→2S1/2 ↔ 32D5/2 transition. Two global laser beams at\n    81→397 nm and 866 nm (not shown in the figure) evenly il\u0002luminate the entire ion chain for Doppler cooling and\n    82→optical pumping, and a tightly focused addressing beam\n    83→at 866 nm allows for resonant excitation to extract the\n    84→397 nm single photons from individual ions. The gener\u0002ated single photons are collected by an objective with\n    85→a numerical aperture of NA = 0.3 (collection efficiency\n    86→Pc ≈ 2.5%) and directed to a 50/50 beam splitter (BS).\n    87→At the exit port of BS, photons are detected by two pho\u0002tomultiplier tube (PMT)-based single-photon detectors\n    88→(SPD), and their arrival times are recorded with a time\u0002tagger for subsequent analysis.\n    89→We generate on-demand single-photons based on a\n    90→background-free scheme, as illustrated in Fig. ??(b) [?\n    91→]. In this process, we first Doppler cool (detuning\n    92→∆ = −Γ/2) the ion chain for 200 µs, with the first 100 µs\n    93→assisted by another beam −500 MHz detuned from the\n    94→4\n    95→2S1/2 ↔ 42P1/2 transition (not shown) to mitigate col\u0002lision induced ion chain melting in the Paul trap [? ].\n    96→Then we begin the photon generation sequences with\n    97→optical pumping to the 3\n    98→2D3/2 state for 3 µs, followed\n    99→by transport of the chain to position each ion in the\n   100→tight focus of the 866 nm addressing beam resonant with\n   101→3\n   102→2D3/2 ↔ 42P1/2 transition to generate 397 nm single\n   103→photons (see Fig. ??(c)).\n   104→In the ion-chain transport process, the endcap volt\u0002ages are controlled by an arbitrary waveform generator\n   105→(AWG) amplified by a custom-made, low-noise amplifier\n   106→circuit with a gain of ten through low-pass filters and\n   107→a vacuum feedthrough. The low-pass filters have cutoff\n   108→frequencies of 1.9 MHz to allow fast transport of the ion\n   109→chain close to the speed of the COM mode frequency.\n   110→The programmed and the measured waveform show a\n   111→negligible latency effect from the filters (Fig. ??(a)). The\n   112→forward shuttling function has eight steps, during each of\n   113→which a different ion is placed in the focus of the address\u0002ing beam for 1.7 µs with the beam turned on simultane\u0002ously. After completing this sequence, we move the entire\n   114→ion chain back to the original position in 35 µs using the\n   115→same function form in one step. The voltage ramping\n   116→function on the two endcaps V1,2(t) is in the form of a\n   117→sigmoid-like polynomial function such that the first and\n   118→second order derivative at the beginning and the end of\n   119→the transport vanish [? ].\n   120→d\n   121→2xi\n   122→dt2\n   123→= −ω\n   124→2\n   125→0xi +\n   126→kq2\n   127→m\n   128→X\n   129→N\n   130→j=1\n   131→j̸=i\n   132→1\n   133→(xi − xj )\n   134→2\n   135→· sgn (xi − xj ) (1)\n   136→where ∆V is the voltage difference between the be\u0002ginning and the end of a step, t is the time after the\n   137→end of the previous step, and T = 9.1 µs is the total\n   138→time of each transport. The details of voltage optimiza\u0002tion and numerical simulation of motional heating can\n   139→be found in [? ]. We reconstruct the temporal profile of\n   140→397 nm photons during transport using the recorded ar\u0002rival times of photons on the PMTs. Fig ??(b) shows the\n   141→emission from individual ions (modes). Data is accumu\u0002lated for 40 min, during which around 1.56×106 attempts\n   142→were made to the whole string, corresponding to attempt\n   143→rate 39.0 kHz, an average photon extraction efficiency of\n   144→0.21 % and single photons count rate of around 71 cps.\n   145→Next, we perform a two-photon correlation experiment\n   146→to test the non-classical characteristics of our multiplexed\n   147→photon source [? ]. The probability of two-photon corre\u0002lation when detecting a correlation event on two detectors\n   148→at different times is given by\n   149→ρc(τ ) = ρ1(τ )ρ2(τ + δT), (2)\n   150→where ρ1(τ ) and ρ2(τ + δT) are the probability of de\u0002tecting a photon at t = τ and τ + δT on detector 1 and\n   151→2. Fig. ??(a) shows the normalized correlation counts as\n   152→a function of the delay mode window. We choose a co\u0002incidence window of 300 ns in each mode and measure\n   153→8 coincident counts at zero delay in 4.8 hours, corre\u0002sponding to g\n   154→(2)(0) = 0.060(13). The residual correla\u0002tion can be explained by excitation of neighboring ions,\n   155→i.e., crosstalk of the addressing beam, which is sepa\u0002rately characterized to be 0.99 % using fluorescence of\n   156→the nine-ion chain on the camera, corresponding to ex\u0002pected average g\n   157→(2)\n   158→exp(0) = 0.049(8) (see Supplemental [?\n   159→]). To further verify this hypothesis, we repeat the mea\u0002surement with a single ion (Fig. ??(c)) and compare\n   160→it to addressing only the fifth ion in a static nine-ion\n   161→chain (Fig. ??(b)). The two experiments yield g\n   162→(2)(0) =\n   163→0.010(6) and g\n   164→(2)(0) = 0.062(17) with 6.0 and 4.8 hours\n   165→of data accumulation, respectively. While the single ion\n   166→g2 is limited by the detector dark counts and ambient\n   167→light, the measurement of the static 9-ion chain g2 is\n   168→limited by the crosstalk of the addressing beam. The\n   169→results indicate the major source of residual correlation\n   170→is addressing crosstalk [? ]. This can be mitigated by\n   171→coupling the single photons into a single-mode fiber or\n   172→improving the optical quality of the excitation beam.\n   173→After characterizing the single-photon nature of the\n   174→transport-multiplexing scheme, we characterize the mo\u0002tion of the ions introduced by shuttling. This is impor\u0002tant as the quality of subsequent quantum operations\n   175→on the ions or ion-photon entanglement will depend on\n   176→\n   177→--- Page 3 ---\n   178→3\n   179→the ions’ motional states. We further explore the sys\u0002tem performance by measuring the motional heating from\n   180→the ion transport. To do this, we first perform side\u0002band cooling for all axial modes sequentially using the\n   181→method similar to that in [? ] and prepare the ion in\n   182→the state |↓⟩ = |4\n   183→2S1/2, mJ = −1/2⟩. We compare the\n   184→|↓⟩ ↔ |↑⟩ = |3\n   185→2D5/2, mJ = −1/2⟩ carrier transition be\u0002fore and after transport with a global 729 nm beam along\n   186→the axial direction to determine how the transport af\u0002fects the ion-motion (Fig. ??). The carrier Rabi flopping\n   187→is motional state sensitive, and the Hamiltonian has the\n   188→form of [? ? ]\n   189→Hi,M = (ℏ/2)Ω(i)σ\n   190→(i)\n   191→x\n   192→Y\n   193→M\n   194→m=1\n   195→exp \u0002iηi,m \u0002am + a\n   196→†\n   197→m\n   198→\u0001\u0003 , (3)\n   199→where Ω\n   200→(i)\n   201→is the Rabi frequency of the ith ion, am and a\n   202→†\n   203→m\n   204→are the creation and annihilation operators on the mth\n   205→mode, and ηi,m is the LD parameter of the ith ion and the\n   206→mth mode. Considering the computational difficulty of\n   207→including all motional modes in the simulation, we only\n   208→consider the COM mode which we expect to be excited\n   209→most because the electric fields, both from the transport\n   210→and surface noise, are correlated over the whole ion string\n   211→(see Supplemental [? ]). Therefore, the average carrier\n   212→Rabi flopping can be simplified to\n   213→Pe(t) = 1\n   214→2N\n   215→\"\n   216→1 −\n   217→X∞\n   218→n=0\n   219→X\n   220→N\n   221→i=0\n   222→Pn cos \u0010Ω\n   223→(i)\n   224→n\n   225→t\n   226→\u0011\n   227→#\n   228→, (4)\n   229→where Pn is the occupation on the nth number state and\n   230→encodes a convolution between a thermal and a coher\u0002ent phonon distribution [? ]. Ω\n   231→(i)\n   232→n is the Rabi frequency\n   233→of the ith ion on the nth number state [? ]. To ver\u0002ify the effectiveness of our approximation, we probe the\n   234→sideband-cooled motional spectrum of the nine-ion chain\n   235→before the tranport and verify that only the COM mode\n   236→is not cooled to near the ground state [? ], for which\n   237→we find a cooling limit of n¯th = 4.0 ± 3.0. We also\n   238→measure the electric-field noise induced heating and find\n   239→a heating rate of 20 quanta / ms.(Fig. ??(a)), indicating\n   240→that the remaining thermal population is likely limited\n   241→by the COM mode heating which scales as ion number\n   242→N [? ]. Fig. ??(b) shows the carrier Rabi flopping after\n   243→ion transport twice as slow as in Fig. ??(a). From nu\u0002merical simulations (blue line), we find that the data can\n   244→be explained well by a coherent state n¯α = |α|\n   245→2 ≈ 50 on\n   246→the COM mode after the transport. Similarly, we per\u0002form the full-speed tranport and the carrier Rabi flop\u0002ping matches with COM coherent state with n¯α ≈ 110\n   247→(Fig. ??(c)). As shown in the Rabi flopping plots, there\n   248→is mismatch between the experimental data and numeri\u0002cal simulation at full speed, which could be due to ther\u0002mal and coherent occupation of other modes and will\n   249→require additional investigation. For example, one can\n   250→use an individual 729 nm addressing beam to probe the\n   251→blue sideband transition of different modes [? ]. The op\u0002timal fast transport of a long ion chain remains an open\n   252→question and is beyond the scope of this work. However,\n   253→we note that further optimization can be done by energy\n   254→self-neutral shuttling [? ? ], implementing closed-loop\n   255→optimization of the shuttling function [? ], etc.\n   256→To summarize, we have presented a multiplexed ion\u0002photon interface by transporting a nine-ion chain with\n   257→synchronized excitation in sub-hundred µs. The speed\n   258→is restricted by the motional frequency and can be in\u0002creased by an order of magnitude, for instance, using a\n   259→3D-printed ion trap [? ] with radial frequency beyond\n   260→10 MHz. The 397 nm photon can be converted to the\n   261→telecommunication band via a two-step QFC [? ]. Once\n   262→integrated with state preparation on 3\n   263→2D3/2 Zeeman sub\u0002level and photon collection with a single mode fiber, we\n   264→expect a faster photon extraction rate [? ] and negligi\u0002ble ion crosstalk while achieving high fidelity ion-photon\n   265→entanglement [? ? ]. Our system can also be combined\n   266→with a miniature cavity [? ] for much higher photon\n   267→extraction efficiency without sacrificing the photon gen\u0002eration rate, while the ion’s positional spread caused by\n   268→coherent excitation can be mitigated by aligning the cav\u0002ity along the radial direction or further optimization of\n   269→the shuttling function. These results stimulate the re\u0002search of fast shuttling of a chain of tens of ions as a\n   270→unit cell of logical qubit with heralded entanglement [?\n   271→] and high-rates entanglement of quantum processors\n   272→across large distances.\n   273→B.Y and Q.W have contributed equally to this work.\n   274→We thank Alp Sipahigil, Ben Lanyon, Tracy Northup,\n   275→You-Wei Hsieh, and Wenji Wu for their helpful discus\u0002sion. Q.W. and H.H. acknowledge funding by the U.S.\n   276→Department of Energy, Office of Science, Office of Basic\n   277→Energy Sciences under Awards No. DE-SC0023277. This\n   278→work is supported by the Office of Science (S.C.) in Ad\u0002vanced Scientific Computing Research (ASCR) through\n   279→FOA - Quantum Internet to Accelerate Scientific Discov\u0002ery (LAB 21-2495) and by NSF Grant No. 2016245.\n   280→∗\n   281→qiming.wu@berkeley.edu\n   282→† hhaeffner@berkeley.edu\n   283→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"uuid":"a3038774-4309-496f-8391-3d9a3fc3bce9","timestamp":"2026-01-29T05:02:55.314Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9ff8561b-6bdd-44d9-96d7-b138e1e41ea7"}
